---

<DocsLayout
  title="Routines"
  subtitle="Saved prompts with schedule or API triggers, scoped per project. Source: packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/routine.ts."
  description="Guide to Nikcli routines, scheduled prompts, API triggers, cron parsing, and project-scoped automation."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="what-are-routines" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>What Are Routines</h2>
    <p>
      A routine pairs a saved prompt with one or more triggers. When a trigger fires, nikcli runs
      the prompt as a background session and records the resulting session ID. Routines are scoped
      to the current project directory — each project has its own routine list.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two trigger types are supported:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>schedule</strong> — a cron expression that fires the routine on a timer.</li>
      <li><strong>api</strong> — a secret token that lets external systems trigger the routine via HTTP.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A single routine can have both a schedule trigger and an API trigger at the same time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="triggers" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Triggers</h2>

    <h3>Schedule trigger</h3>
    <p>
      The schedule trigger accepts the following cron patterns:
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Pattern</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>@hourly</code></td><td>Once per hour.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@daily</code></td><td>Once per day.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>@weekly</code></td><td>Once per week.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>*/N</code></td><td>Every N minutes (e.g. <code>*/30</code> = every 30 minutes).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>0 */N * * *</code></td><td>Every N hours (e.g. <code>0 */6 * * *</code> = every 6 hours).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Unrecognised cron patterns">
      <p>
        If the cron pattern you supply does not match one of the supported forms above, the routine
        is created and saved but the schedule will <strong>not</strong> fire. nikcli prints a warning
        at creation time and the trigger is stored as-is. You can still run the routine manually
        with <code>nikcli routine run &lt;id&gt;</code>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>

    <h3>API trigger</h3>
    <p>
      API triggers attach a secret token to the routine. Any system that can make an HTTP POST
      request can fire the routine without needing direct access to the machine running nikcli.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      POST /mobile/routines/trigger/&lt;token&gt;
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The token is generated automatically when you pass <code>--api</code> to
      <code>routine create</code>, or you can supply your own with <code>--api-token</code>.
      nikcli prints the full endpoint URL after the routine is created.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="subcommands" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Subcommands</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Subcommand</th>
          <th>Alias</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine list</code></td>
          <td><code>ls</code></td>
          <td>List all routines for the current project. Accepts <code>--format table|json</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine create</code></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Create a routine interactively or via flags. See flags below.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine get &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Show full details of a routine. Accepts <code>--format text|json</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine run &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Trigger an immediate run. Accepts <code>--text</code> to append one-off context to this run.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine pause &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Pause the routine; disables all scheduled triggers until resumed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine resume &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Resume a paused routine and re-enable its triggers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>routine delete &lt;id&gt;</code></td>
          <td><code>rm</code></td>
          <td>Delete the routine. Prompts for confirmation unless <code>--yes</code> / <code>-y</code> is passed.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="creating-a-routine" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Creating a Routine</h2>
    <p>
      Run <code>nikcli routine create</code> without flags to go through an interactive prompt for
      name, prompt, and cron schedule. Pass flags to skip the interactive steps:
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Flag</th>
          <th>Alias</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--name</code></td><td><code>-n</code></td><td>Routine name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--prompt</code></td><td><code>-p</code></td><td>Prompt text to run.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--cron</code></td><td>—</td><td>Cron schedule expression (see supported patterns above).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--api</code></td><td>—</td><td>Enable an API trigger with a randomly generated token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--api-token</code></td><td>—</td><td>Enable an API trigger with a specific custom token.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      # Interactive creation
      nikcli routine create

      # Fully scripted — daily schedule + API trigger
      nikcli routine create \
        --name "daily-review" \
        --prompt "Review recent changes and summarize any risks." \
        --cron @daily \
        --api

      # Every 30 minutes, custom API token
      nikcli routine create \
        --name "health-check" \
        --prompt "Run the test suite and report failures." \
        --cron "*/30" \
        --api-token "my-secret-token"
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="api-trigger" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>API Trigger</h2>
    <p>
      After creating a routine with <code>--api</code> or <code>--api-token</code>, nikcli prints
      the trigger endpoint. Fire it from any HTTP client:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      # Trigger a routine from a CI pipeline or webhook
      curl -X POST https://your-host/mobile/routines/trigger/nkr_abc123...

      # View the token again
      nikcli routine get &lt;id&gt;
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Token prefix">
      <p>
        Automatically generated tokens start with <code>nkr_</code> followed by 64 hex characters.
        Custom tokens passed via <code>--api-token</code> are stored as-is.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="lifecycle" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Lifecycle</h2>
    <p>
      A routine moves through the following states:
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>active</code></td><td>Triggers are enabled and will fire on schedule or API call.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>paused</code></td><td>Triggers are disabled. The routine can still be run manually with <code>routine run</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      nikcli routine list
      nikcli routine pause rtn_abc123
      nikcli routine resume rtn_abc123
      nikcli routine run rtn_abc123 --text "focus on the auth module"
      nikcli routine delete rtn_abc123 --yes
    </DocCode>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
